1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,059
Lesson 7.5.

2
00:00:02,439 --> 00:00:04,688
Trigonometric sum identities.

3
00:00:05,817 --> 00:00:09,664
When the same quantity can be
computed in two different ways,

4
00:00:09,664 --> 00:00:13,133
equating the two answers
produces an identity for free.

5
00:00:13,513 --> 00:00:17,704
The probability that the walk
returns to the identity is one of

6
00:00:17,704 --> 00:00:21,496
those quantities: one can
count paths or sum eigenvalues.

7
00:00:21,876 --> 00:00:24,972
Today we obtain
trigonometric sum identities

8
00:00:24,972 --> 00:00:27,646
this way, some classical and some new.

9
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:32,455
In exponent coordinates,
each step adds zero with

10
00:00:32,455 --> 00:00:36,689
probability one half, or ±
2^t with a small probability.

11
00:00:37,069 --> 00:00:42,243
After ℓ steps we have a total
exponent E, any integer, not reduced.

12
00:00:42,623 --> 00:00:47,154
The walk is at the identity
exactly when E is a multiple of r.

13
00:00:47,534 --> 00:00:51,000
So the return probability
is the sum of the

14
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,627
probabilities of E = j
r over all integers j.

15
00:00:55,007 --> 00:00:59,275
The remarkable part: the distribution
of E does not depend on r; the order

16
00:00:59,275 --> 00:01:03,140
only appears at the end, when asking
which exponents are multiples.

17
00:01:04,250 --> 00:01:08,260
The other form is the one
from lesson 7.3: the return

18
00:01:08,260 --> 00:01:12,725
probability is the average of
λ_k^ℓ over the r frequencies.

19
00:01:13,105 --> 00:01:18,107
The heat kernel is unique: both
expressions describe the same probability.

20
00:01:18,487 --> 00:01:21,546
Therefore, the
combinatorial sum equals the

21
00:01:21,546 --> 00:01:24,178
spectral sum for every r and every ℓ.

22
00:01:25,283 --> 00:01:29,644
Let's see it in the simplest
case: the cycle with ± 1 jumps.

23
00:01:30,024 --> 00:01:33,696
Its eigenvalue is cos²(πk/r).

24
00:01:34,076 --> 00:01:37,855
And a lazy step is equivalent
to two half steps of

25
00:01:37,855 --> 00:01:41,180
± ½, so the displacement
follows a binomial.

26
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:49,208
The identity that comes out is (1/r) Σ
cos^(2ℓ)(πk/r) = 4^(−ℓ) Σ C(2ℓ, ℓ + j r).

27
00:01:49,588 --> 00:01:57,091
With r = 5 and ℓ = 3, both sides
equal twenty over sixty-four: 0.3125.

28
00:01:58,217 --> 00:02:01,519
With the dyadic generators we obtain a new

29
00:02:01,519 --> 00:02:04,665
lacunary identity: the average of λ_k^ℓ,

30
00:02:05,045 --> 00:02:10,162
where each λ_k is an average of
cosines of 2πk2^t/r, equals the

31
00:02:10,162 --> 00:02:14,873
probability that a sum of
dyadic jumps is a multiple of r.

32
00:02:15,253 --> 00:02:19,769
For r = 33 and ten steps, the
combinatorial side, computed as an exact

33
00:02:19,769 --> 00:02:24,608
fraction with seven thousand seven
hundred thirty-seven possible exponents,

34
00:02:24,988 --> 00:02:28,647
equals 0.035836548, and the spectral side

35
00:02:28,647 --> 00:02:32,218
agrees to the last digit of the machine.

36
00:02:33,350 --> 00:02:34,302
What are they for?

37
00:02:34,682 --> 00:02:37,985
First, as proof: the
combinatorial form shows that the

38
00:02:37,985 --> 00:02:41,410
value is a rational number
with a known denominator, and

39
00:02:41,410 --> 00:02:44,530
the spectral form, that it
converges to one over r.

40
00:02:44,910 --> 00:02:49,173
Second, as a check: any
simulation or diffusion hardware

41
00:02:49,173 --> 00:02:53,055
must reproduce these values
exactly in small cases.

42
00:02:53,435 --> 00:02:58,060
Third, as a tool: lacunary sums
are hard to bound directly,

43
00:02:58,060 --> 00:03:02,214
and the walk interpretation
gives estimates for free.

44
00:03:03,317 --> 00:03:06,817
Use them as unit tests for
diffusion simulators and

45
00:03:06,817 --> 00:03:10,111
prototypes, recognize in
them classical binomial

46
00:03:10,111 --> 00:03:14,709
multisection identities, and bound
lacunary sums with mixing times.

47
00:03:15,089 --> 00:03:18,278
In lab ten you will verify both forms.

48
00:03:19,383 --> 00:03:23,048
Common mistakes: reducing the
exponent modulo r at every

49
00:03:23,048 --> 00:03:26,581
step, which requires knowing
the order from the start.

50
00:03:26,961 --> 00:03:28,517
Forgetting the lazy weight.

51
00:03:28,897 --> 00:03:31,829
Mixing the eigenvalues of P and W.

52
00:03:32,209 --> 00:03:36,794
And checking only in floating point,
when exact fractions are better.

53
00:03:37,917 --> 00:03:42,404
In summary: the return probability
is computed by counting jump sums

54
00:03:42,404 --> 00:03:46,495
that are multiples of r or by
averaging powers of eigenvalues.

55
00:03:46,875 --> 00:03:50,669
Since the heat kernel is unique,
both agree, and that gives

56
00:03:50,669 --> 00:03:54,335
exact trigonometric identities,
from the classical one of

57
00:03:54,335 --> 00:03:57,486
cosines and binomials to
the dyadic lacunary one.

58
00:03:58,617 --> 00:04:03,152
To practice: verify the cycle
identity by hand in a small case.

59
00:04:03,532 --> 00:04:07,337
Explain the equivalence of the
lazy step with two half steps.

60
00:04:07,717 --> 00:04:10,204
Find the denominator of
the combinatorial side.

61
00:04:10,584 --> 00:04:13,227
And say which side shows
the limit immediately.
